{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"**No—not by itself.** As of November 6, 2026, an ordinary U.S. ’40 Act ETF is legally separate from its adviser/sponsor. Its portfolio belongs to the fund and is held in custody for it; shareholders own fund shares. The sponsor’s creditors generally cannot seize that portfolio merely because the sponsor becomes insolvent, nor does that insolvency automatically cancel or render ETF shares worthless.\n\nThat is **asset separation, not a guaranteed outcome in every insolvency**. Sponsor failure can disrupt operations, necessitate a replacement adviser, or lead to fund liquidation. Liquidation generally distributes remaining net assets after fund liabilities and expenses; it does not guarantee recovery of the investment’s original value. Fraud, disputed ownership, valid liens, or other exceptional facts can complicate outcomes.\n\n**Worth knowing:** Protection from the sponsor’s creditors is not protection from investment losses or the fund’s own liabilities."}}
